---
import DocsLayout from '../../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro';

const title = 'Python uncertainties vs Measurements.jl vs Sounio | Sounio';
const description = "Side-by-side comparison of three ways to propagate measurement uncertainty: Python's uncertainties, Julia's Measurements.jl and Sounio's Knowledge<T> type.";
const heading = 'Sounio vs Python uncertainties vs Measurements.jl';

const author = {
  '@type': 'Person',
  name: 'Demetrios Chiuratto Agourakis',
  url: 'https://www.souniolang.org/about/',
  sameAs: [
    'https://orcid.org/0009-0001-8671-8878',
    'https://github.com/agourakis82',
  ],
};

const about = [
  { '@type': 'Thing', name: 'measurement uncertainty' },
  { '@type': 'Thing', name: 'GUM' },
  { '@type': 'Thing', name: 'uncertainty propagation' },
];

const breadcrumbs = [
  { name: 'Learn', href: '/learn/' },
  { name: 'Epistemic', href: '/learn/epistemic/' },
  { name: 'Tools compared', href: '/learn/epistemic/uncertainty-tools-compared/' },
];

const faq = [
  {
    q: 'Is there a Python uncertainties alternative?',
    a: 'Measurements.jl is the Julia library for the same job: linear propagation and functional correlation, documented in its own examples. Sounio is not a wrapper around either library. Uncertainty is the type Knowledge<T> in a research language, Sounio 3.0 (pre-release). This page does not survey other Python packages.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Do uncertainties and Measurements.jl handle correlation?',
    a: 'Yes for functional correlation. A ufloat minus itself printed 0.0+/-0 when this page ran it. The Measurements.jl examples page shows x − x as 0.0 ± 0.0; that line was not re-run here. Both guides also document correlated inputs from a covariance matrix. Those matrix calls were not re-run.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What does Sounio add?',
    a: 'The tests declare the Epistemic effect on the function that calls measure, variance_of and correlate, so the signature shows that the function touches uncertain data. On Madaros, at commit 3d3da0806, variance_of(x + y) printed 0.050000, then 0.090000 after correlate(x, y, 1.0). See known limits. Engines were not compared here.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Which one should I use for my lab data today?',
    a: 'If the pipeline is already Python or Julia, keep the library. Sounio 3.0 (pre-release) is for exploring uncertainty as a type, and for research on that idea. It is not a replacement for a laboratory pipeline, and this page does not call it production software.',
  },
];

const schema = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'FAQPage',
  mainEntity: faq.map((item) => ({
    '@type': 'Question',
    name: item.q,
    acceptedAnswer: { '@type': 'Answer', text: item.a },
  })),
};

const headings = [
  { id: 'quick-comparison', text: 'Quick comparison', depth: 2 },
  { id: 'same-problem', text: 'Same rectangle in three languages', depth: 2 },
  { id: 'correlations', text: 'Correlations', depth: 2 },
  { id: 'library-vs-language', text: 'Library vs language-level', depth: 2 },
  { id: 'when-to-pick', text: 'When to pick which', depth: 2 },
  { id: 'limits', text: 'Limits', depth: 2 },
  { id: 'faq', text: 'FAQ', depth: 2 },
];

const pythonArea = `from uncertainties import ufloat

L = ufloat(10.0, 0.1)
W = ufloat(5.0, 0.05)
A = L * W
print(A)
print(A.nominal_value)
print(A.std_dev)
print(A.std_dev ** 2)`;

const pythonDiff = `x = ufloat(5, 0.5)
y = ufloat(5, 0.5)
print(x - x)
print(x - y)`;

const juliaArea = `using Measurements

L = 10.0 ± 0.1
W = 5.0 ± 0.05
A = L * W`;

const sounioArea = `let k_comprimento: Knowledge<f64> = measure(10.0, uncertainty: 0.1)
let k_largura: Knowledge<f64> = measure(5.0, uncertainty: 0.05)
let comp = k_comprimento.value
let larg = k_largura.value
let area = comp * larg
let var_area = variance_of(area)`;

const sounioCorrelate = `let kx: Knowledge<f64> = measure(1.0, uncertainty: 0.1)
let ky: Knowledge<f64> = measure(2.0, uncertainty: 0.2)
let x = kx.value
let y = ky.value

let v_ind = variance_of(x + y)

let _r = correlate(x, y, 1.0)
let v_cor = variance_of(x + y)`;
---

<DocsLayout title={title} description={description} heading={heading} headings={headings} schema={schema} localizedAlternates={false} breadcrumbs={breadcrumbs} dateModified="2026-11-06" articleHeadline={heading} articleAbout={about} articleAuthor={author}>
  <p>
    For day-to-day scientific code, Measurements.jl and Python’s <code>uncertainties</code> are the mature libraries. Sounio 3.0 (pre-release) is a research language that puts the same first-order propagation in the type <code>Knowledge&lt;T&gt;</code>, with correlation declared by <code>correlate</code>. All three follow linear propagation and can track functional correlation, so the same value minus itself has zero uncertainty. This page does not rank them for speed or accuracy.
  </p>
  <p>
    Cells below are filled only from a run on 6 November 2026 or from a document fetched that day. Anything else says <strong>not verified</strong>. Sounio runs used Madaros, the default engine, at commit <code>3d3da0806</code>. No Sounio release number is stated here. Python <code>uncertainties</code> was executed for the two snippets in this page; its package version is omitted. Julia was not installed, so Measurements.jl figures are the ones printed in the stable docs, marked as documentation output.
  </p>
  <p>
    The GUM is JCGM 100:2008, the guide to the expression of uncertainty in measurement (<a href="https://www.bipm.org/en/committees/cg/jcgm/publications">BIPM</a>). The three tools are prior art beside one another: Measurements.jl’s introduction describes linear propagation and credits the functional-correlation method in Giordano, arXiv:1610.08716. Python <code>uncertainties</code> is Eric O. Lebigot’s library.
  </p>

  <h2 id="quick-comparison">Quick comparison</h2>
  <p>
    Read a cell as a sourced fact, not as a winner. “Documented” means the linked page says it. “Run here” means this environment printed it on 6 November 2026.
  </p>
  <div class="compare-wrap">
    <table class="compare-table">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th></th>
          <th>Python <code>uncertainties</code></th>
          <th>Measurements.jl</th>
          <th>Sounio</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <th>Ecosystem</th>
          <td>Library in the Python scientific stack. User guide fetched 6 November 2026.</td>
          <td>Library in Julia. Stable introduction and examples fetched the same day.</td>
          <td>Research language, Sounio 3.0 (pre-release). Not a drop-in laboratory stack.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th>Propagation</th>
          <td>Linear error propagation. The <a href="https://uncertainties.readthedocs.io/en/latest/user_guide.html">user guide</a> says the squared uncertainty is the sum of squared contributions from that theory.</td>
          <td>Linear error propagation. The <a href="https://juliaphysics.github.io/Measurements.jl/stable/">stable introduction</a> says that theory is what the package uses.</td>
          <td>GUM first-order, including a covariance term when <code>correlate</code> is called. Monte Carlo is not claimed.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th>Functional correlation (x − x = 0)</th>
          <td>Run here: <code>ufloat(5, 0.5) - itself</code> printed <code>0.0+/-0</code>. The user guide states the same rule.</td>
          <td>Examples page prints <code>x - x</code> as <code>0.0 ± 0.0</code> for <code>8.4 ± 0.7</code>. Documentation output, not re-run.</td>
          <td>Run here: <a href="https://github.com/Sounio-lang/sounio/blob/main/tests/run-pass/gum_correlated.sio"><code>gum_correlated.sio</code></a> printed <code>var(h-h)=0.000000</code>.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th>External covariance input</th>
          <td>Documented. The user guide section “Use of a covariance matrix” builds correlated variables from nominal values plus a covariance matrix. Not re-run.</td>
          <td>Documented. The <a href="https://juliaphysics.github.io/Measurements.jl/stable/examples/">examples page</a> shows <code>Measurements.correlated_values</code> taking a covariance matrix from a fit. Not re-run.</td>
          <td>Pairwise <code>correlate(x, y, rho)</code> is in the test below, with ρ written as a literal in that call. A constructor that ingests a whole covariance matrix: <strong>not verified</strong>.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th>Covariance output</th>
          <td>Documented. <code>covariance_matrix</code> and, with NumPy, <code>correlation_matrix</code>. Not re-run.</td>
          <td>Documented. <code>Measurements.cov</code> and <code>Measurements.cor</code> on the examples page. Not re-run.</td>
          <td><code>variance_of</code> prints one variance. A covariance-matrix export: <strong>not verified</strong>.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th>Units</th>
          <td><strong>not verified</strong> (Pint was not run, and the user guide fetched here does not specify a Pint mode).</td>
          <td>The stable introduction says external packages can carry physical units with the measurement. Unitful by name: <strong>not verified</strong>.</td>
          <td>Partial. <a href="/learn/units/">Units of measure</a> still asks for a conservative description of end-to-end enforcement. <code>Knowledge&lt;m&gt;</code> is a separate example and was not re-run here.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th>Visible at compile time</th>
          <td>Runtime object, <code>ufloat</code>. The user guide does not describe a compile-time effect.</td>
          <td>Library type <code>Measurement</code>. The stable docs do not describe an effect in a function signature.</td>
          <td>Language type <code>Knowledge&lt;T&gt;</code>. The programs below declare <code>with … Epistemic</code>. A missing-effect refusal was not re-run here.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th>Arrays</th>
          <td><strong>not verified</strong>. The user-guide contents name a numpy-arrays page; that page was not checked, and no array program was run.</td>
          <td>The stable introduction says you can define arrays of measurements and that some linear-algebra functions work. Not re-run.</td>
          <td><strong>not verified</strong>.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th>Known limits</th>
          <td>The guide’s method is linear. Further limits: <strong>not verified</strong> on this page.</td>
          <td>Functional correlation and <code>correlated_values</code> are in the examples. Further limits: <strong>not verified</strong> on this page.</td>
          <td>KL-11 and KL-15, linked below. First-order only in the tests cited. Research software.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th>License</th>
          <td><a href="https://github.com/lmfit/uncertainties/blob/master/LICENSE.txt">BSD 3-clause</a> (<code>LICENSE.txt</code>, fetched 6 November 2026).</td>
          <td>MIT Expat, as stated on the stable introduction. Original author Mosè Giordano.</td>
          <td><a href="https://github.com/Sounio-lang/sounio/blob/main/LICENSE">Apache-2.0</a>.</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2 id="same-problem">Same rectangle in three languages</h2>
  <p>
    Independent sides 10.0 ± 0.1 and 5.0 ± 0.05. The first-order law for independent inputs gives variance (5.0 × 0.1)² + (10.0 × 0.05)² = 0.25 + 0.25 = 0.50, and a standard uncertainty of √0.50. That identity is the shared formula. It is not a benchmark.
  </p>
  <p>Python, run here:</p>
  <pre><code>{pythonArea}</code></pre>
  <pre><code>{`50.0+/-0.7
50.0
0.7071067811865476
0.5000000000000001`}</code></pre>
  <p>
    The one-line print <code>50.0+/-0.7</code> is the library’s short display. The standard deviation printed in full is 0.7071067811865476, and its square is 0.5000000000000001, the floating-point form of 0.50. Package version omitted on purpose.
  </p>
  <p>
    Julia, the pattern from the Measurements.jl docs (<code>measurement</code> or the <code>±</code> operator). This product was not run. No output is invented for it.
  </p>
  <pre><code>{juliaArea}</code></pre>
  <p>
    Sounio, excerpt of <a href="https://github.com/Sounio-lang/sounio/blob/main/demos/intro/demo_incerteza.sio"><code>demos/intro/demo_incerteza.sio</code></a>. The file’s own comment records the same 0.50. Run here, Madaros at <code>3d3da0806</code>, the program printed:
  </p>
  <pre><code>{sounioArea}</code></pre>
  <pre><code>{`Área        = 50.000000 ± 0.707107 (Variância calculada: 0.500000)`}</code></pre>
  <p>
    <code>print_f64</code> stops at six digits, so 0.707107 is the printed standard uncertainty of a variance the same run printed as 0.500000. The surrounding lines of that demo are in Portuguese because the source prints Portuguese. The perimeter line from the same run is a different quantity and is not the correlation example below.
  </p>

  <h2 id="correlations">Correlations</h2>
  <p>
    Functional correlation is the x − x case: one uncertain value used twice. External correlation is a relationship you supply between two different inputs.
  </p>
  <p>Python, run here. Two separate <code>ufloat</code> calls are independent, which is what the user guide says.</p>
  <pre><code>{pythonDiff}</code></pre>
  <pre><code>{`0.0+/-0
0.0+/-0.7`}</code></pre>
  <p>
    Measurements.jl’s <a href="https://juliaphysics.github.io/Measurements.jl/stable/examples/">examples</a> print <code>x - x</code> as <code>0.0 ± 0.0</code>, and a separately constructed value with the same nominal figures gives a non-zero uncertainty (<code>(x + x) - v</code> prints <code>0.0 ± 2.0</code> in that listing). Those lines are documentation output. They were not re-run. The same examples page documents <code>Measurements.cov</code>, <code>Measurements.cor</code>, and <code>Measurements.correlated_values</code> for a covariance matrix. This page does not claim that matrix support is missing.
  </p>
  <p>
    Sounio, same variable, run here. <code>gum_correlated.sio</code> measures h as 1.75 ± 0.01 and prints <code>var(h-h)=0.000000</code> and <code>var(h*h)=0.001225</code> (the file’s comment: 4·h²·var(h), not the independent 2·h²·var(h)).
  </p>
  <p>
    Two different inputs use <code>correlate</code>. Excerpt of <a href="https://github.com/Sounio-lang/sounio/blob/main/tests/run-pass/madaros_gum_fo_correlate.sio"><code>tests/run-pass/madaros_gum_fo_correlate.sio</code></a>. The file requires Madaros. With σ = 0.1 and 0.2, the independent variance of x + y is 0.05 and the fully correlated variance is 0.09 = (σx + σy)².
  </p>
  <pre><code>{sounioCorrelate}</code></pre>
  <pre><code>{`v_ind=0.050000
v_cor=0.090000`}</code></pre>
  <p>
    Those two lines were printed by Madaros at commit <code>3d3da0806</code> on 6 November 2026, both in a check recorded against this file and in the re-run for this page. The same run also printed <code>MADAROS_GUM_FO_CORRELATE_PASS</code>. The ρ in the call is the literal <code>1.0</code>. Whether a non-literal ρ is refused was not re-run here.
  </p>

  <h2 id="library-vs-language">Library vs language-level</h2>
  <p>
    <code>uncertainties</code> and Measurements.jl attach the uncertainty to a value at run time. You import a library, construct a number, and the arithmetic on that number carries the error bar. Nothing in the guides fetched for this page puts an effect name in the function type.
  </p>
  <p>
    Sounio’s tests write the effect in the signature. <code>madaros_gum_fo_correlate.sio</code> opens with <code>fn main() -&gt; i64 with IO, Mut, Div, Epistemic</code>. <code>demo_incerteza.sio</code> opens with <code>fn main() with IO, Mut, Div, Epistemic</code>. A reader of the source can see which function is allowed to touch a measured value. How a missing <code>with</code> clause is rejected is the subject of the <a href="/learn/effects/">effect system</a> page; that refusal was not re-run for this comparison. There is no timing comparison between the library call and the effect.
  </p>
  <p>
    The wider guide to the type, separate from this comparison, is <a href="/learn/epistemic/uncertainty/">uncertainty</a>. The epistemic overview is <a href="/learn/epistemic/">/learn/epistemic/</a>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="when-to-pick">When to pick which</h2>
  <p>
    Keep Python <code>uncertainties</code> when the rest of the work is Python. Keep Measurements.jl when the rest of the work is Julia. Both are the libraries this page treated as the established answers, and both already document functional correlation and a covariance-matrix input.
  </p>
  <p>
    Pick up Sounio 3.0 (pre-release) when the question is whether the compiler should see the uncertainty: a <code>Knowledge&lt;T&gt;</code> value and an <code>Epistemic</code> effect in the signature. That is a research use. It is not a reason to leave a working laboratory pipeline.
  </p>
  <p>
    Against mp-units and physure, rather than against these two libraries, see <a href="/learn/epistemic/sounio-vs-mp-units-vs-physure/">units + correlated uncertainty (mp-units, physure)</a>. Pint combined with <code>uncertainties</code> is <strong>not verified</strong> here.
  </p>

  <h2 id="limits">Limits</h2>
  <p>
    Sounio 3.0 is pre-release research software. The open ledger is <a href="https://github.com/Sounio-lang/sounio/blob/main/docs/compiler/KNOWN_LIMITATIONS.md">KNOWN_LIMITATIONS.md</a>, also linked from <a href="/honesty/">honesty</a> and <a href="/learn/feature-status/">feature status</a>.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>KL-11: first-order channels do not cross user calls in general. The same section records a narrower case, pure helpers, where the lowering carries the channel. This page did not re-measure that boundary.</li>
    <li>KL-15: no <code>Knowledge&lt;f128&gt;</code>, and no GUM over <code>f128</code>.</li>
    <li>Monte Carlo propagation for Sounio: <strong>not verified</strong>. Not claimed.</li>
    <li>Units together with <code>Knowledge&lt;T&gt;</code> on every path: partial, as <a href="/learn/units/">/learn/units/</a> states.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    The Python and Julia limits named above are only the ones those guides state in the pages that were fetched. A short display such as <code>50.0+/-0.7</code> is a printing choice in the Python run, not evidence that one tool is closer to √0.50 than another.
  </p>
  <p>
    Small programs can be tried in the <a href="/playground/">playground</a>. The numbers on this page come from the commands below, not from that editor.
  </p>
  <pre><code>{`export SOUNIO_STDLIB_PATH="$(pwd)/stdlib"
./bin/souc run demos/intro/demo_incerteza.sio
./bin/souc run tests/run-pass/madaros_gum_fo_correlate.sio
./bin/souc run tests/run-pass/gum_correlated.sio`}</code></pre>

  <h2 id="faq">FAQ</h2>
  {faq.map((item) => (
    <div>
      <h3>{item.q}</h3>
      <p>{item.a}</p>
    </div>
  ))}
